Reach the fallback for DECRQM on modes libvterm does not model

request_dec_mode() answers an unrecognised mode with "0" -- not
recognised -- and returns, so vterm_screen_set_unrecognised_fallbacks
never sees the query. This is the same defect 0002 fixed for
set_dec_mode, on the other half of the same feature, and in practice it
undoes that fix: 0002 lets an application implement DEC 2026 on top of
libvterm, and this then tells every client that asks that the terminal
does not support it.

That is worse than not answering. A client probes with DECRQM precisely
so it can use a feature without guessing from TERM; told "0" it takes the
terminal at its word, falls back, and the implementation behind it is
never reached.

The fallback is handed the query in its own shape -- leader "?",
intermediate "$", command 'p' -- so an application matches on exactly
what it would have matched on had the sequence arrived unrecognised in
the first place. Only when the fallback declines does libvterm send its
own "0".

The consult is at the TOP of the function rather than in the default
branch, matching 0002 and for the same second reason: an application must
be able to answer for a mode libvterm DOES model, or it cannot decline
one. Reporting "2 -- recognised but reset" for a feature the layer above
does not implement leaves a client believing the mode exists and can be
enabled; only "0 -- not recognised" turns the capability off. Those mean
different things and clients act on the difference.

Applied on top of 0001-0003 at build time (see the top-level Makefile);
upstream is otherwise untouched. Worth sending upstream alongside 0002.

--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -869,6 +884,22 @@ static void set_dec_mode(VTermState *state, int num, int val)
 
 static void request_dec_mode(VTermState *state, int num)
 {
+  /* Offer the query to the application-level fallback before answering.
+   *
+   * At the top rather than in the default branch, for the reason 0002
+   * gives about the other half of this feature: a mode libvterm models
+   * would otherwise be answered from libvterm's own state, and an
+   * application that has declined that mode cannot say so. "2" --
+   * recognised but reset -- leaves a client believing the mode exists;
+   * only "0" turns the capability off.
+   *
+   * An application that declines gets libvterm's own answer, unchanged. */
+  if(state->fallbacks && state->fallbacks->csi) {
+    long fallback_args[1] = { num };
+    if((*state->fallbacks->csi)("?", fallback_args, 1, "$", 'p', state->fbdata))
+      return;
+  }
+
   int reply;
 
   switch(num) {
